<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <!-- netstandard2.0 is the only TFM referenceable from both a legacy net48 spec project
             (typeof() over net48 product types) and the net10 CLI/Roslyn/tests. It forces the
             desirable Core(model)/Roslyn split. LangVersion 13.0 is pinned
             because netstandard2.0 otherwise defaults to C# 7.3, losing primary constructors,
             file-scoped namespaces, and get-only auto-props set from a primary-ctor param
             (which need no IsExternalInit — so no records/init, no PolySharp shim). -->
        <TargetFramework>netstandard2.0</TargetFramework>
        <LangVersion>13.0</LangVersion>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>

        <!-- The spec-contract package: what a spec project references. PackageId is explicit for
             clarity; version + shared metadata come from the repo-root Directory.Build.props. -->
        <PackageId>Zphil.LoadBearing</PackageId>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
        <Description>The LoadBearing spec contract: a fluent C# builder and reified rule model for describing .NET architecture — layers, scopes, and rules, each with a posture (Enforce for the law, Migrate for ratcheted debt with a grandfathered baseline, Quarantine for code contained rather than touched). Architecture spec projects reference this package; checking and rendering come from the loadbearing tool (Zphil.LoadBearing.Cli) or the xUnit adapter (Zphil.LoadBearing.Xunit). netstandard2.0 with zero dependencies, so a spec project can target anything from net48 up.</Description>
        <PackageTags>architecture;architecture-as-code;fitness-functions;dependency-rules;layering;tech-debt;legacy;csharp;dotnet</PackageTags>

        <!-- ApiCompat, at pack time, against the bytes actually on nuget.org — not against a text
             file this repo also writes, which can only say what someone last approved. A published
             NuGet version can never be replaced, only unlisted, and this is the contract package
             every consumer's spec compiles against, so the moment worth checking is the moment the
             nupkg is built. The SDK loads the comparison task from inside itself and fetches the
             baseline with a PackageDownload, which is a different MSBuild item from
             PackageReference — so the self-spec rule packaging/core-carries-nothing stays green and
             the nuspec <dependencies> stays empty, which is what lets both READMEs keep claiming
             zero dependencies.

             Strict mode compares in both directions, so an ADDITION reports as well as a removal.
             That is the half worth paying for here: a removal breaks a consumer loudly at their next
             build, while an addition is the thing that silently becomes permanent. It is also what
             lets the committed surface pin in tests/ stay at type-and-enum grain rather than
             enumerating every member: with member additions reported here, pinning them there would
             move that file on roughly half of all commits to say the same thing twice.

             What this check does NOT give is locality. It runs at pack time, which in practice is one
             CI job and the release gate, so the pin exists to red in the suite beside the edit. An
             enum renumbering reports here as CP0011 and without strict mode, being a break rather
             than a growth (measured 2026-09-12) — but it is also the one surface change that breaks
             no compile anywhere, so it is the one most worth catching in seconds.

             The baseline lags <Version> by design: it names the last PUBLISHED version, so between
             releases it sits equal to <Version> and the accumulated delta is visible. Bumping it is
             part of the release ritual, deliberately NOT a fifth site in ManifestVersionTests'
             four-way version equality; PublicSurfaceSyncTests asserts only that it is no newer.

             Acknowledgement is CompatibilitySuppressions.xml, regenerated with
             -p:ApiCompatGenerateSuppressionFile=true and reviewed per symbol. -->
        <EnablePackageValidation>true</EnablePackageValidation>
        <PackageValidationBaselineVersion>0.7.0</PackageValidationBaselineVersion>
        <EnableStrictModeForBaselineValidation>true</EnableStrictModeForBaselineValidation>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!-- The Roslyn extraction project mints and fills Codebase nodes (internal ctors/setters);
             it is machinery-only and never enters Core's dependency closure. `loadbearing` is the CLI —
             its AssemblyName, not its project name, which is what an InternalsVisibleTo entry has to
             match — and it is here for the internal rendering helpers Core owns (count inflection), so
             the English both render targets pin has one owner rather than a copy per target. -->
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Zphil.LoadBearing.Roslyn" />
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="loadbearing" />
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Zphil.LoadBearing.Tests" />
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DynamicProxyGenAssembly2" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
